## 1. What you need

You need Linux on x86_64, `git`, and host `gcc`.
The first install is a host script. It is not `.oo`.
You cannot run `.oo` until `ooda` is on the machine.

The tools the script places are `ooda` and `oodac`.

Mac, Windows, and Linux ARM are not this install. See [How it works](Limits.oot).

## 2. Install

First machine:

```
curl -fsSL https://openooda.org/install | bash
```

You can download the script and read it first:

```
curl -fsSL https://openooda.org/install -o ooda-install
bash ooda-install
```

The script looks at the OS and CPU. On Linux x86_64 it clones the tree, builds the CLI if needed, and copies `ooda`, `oodac`, `std`, and `runtime` under `~/.local`. It writes `~/.config/ooda/env`.

```
. ~/.config/ooda/env
ooda version
ooda run ~/.local/share/ooda/share/fixtures/hello.oo
```

If you already have a checkout with `bin/ooda` and `oodac/oodac`:

```
OODA_FS_WRITEDIR=$HOME OODA_CHECKOUT=$PWD ooda install
. ~/.config/ooda/env
ooda version
```

`ooda install` is the checkout copy. It is not the first-machine path.

## 3. Write hello.oo

Save this as `hello.oo`:

```
pub fn greet(name: String) -> String {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

verify greet {
    assert_eq!(greet("World"), "Hello, World!");
}

pub fn main() {
    println(greet("openOODA"));
}
```

`pub fn` is a public function.
`verify` is a test block next to the function.
`main` is the entry you run.

Read [Language](Language.oot) for more of the grammar.
An effect needs a capability token. That story is on [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 4. Check, run, test

Typecheck only:

```
ooda check hello.oo
```

Typecheck, emit C, compile with `gcc`, run:

```
ooda run hello.oo
```

`ooda run` prints the program and skips `verify`.

Run `verify` and asserts:

```
ooda test hello.oo
```

`ooda test` runs `verify` and sets `OODA_TEST=1`.
A failing assert fails the command.

JSON errors for an editor or a script:

```
ooda check hello.oo --json-errors
```

`check` is the fast loop.
The full command list is on [Commands](Options.oot).

## 5. Fix a file

`ooda fix` patches these error classes:

- missing import
- missing `}`
- type errors
- unused `Result`
- discarded `Result` wrap

It asks unless you pass `--yes`.
Writes go through `write_file`.
You need `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
Absolute paths and symlinks are refused.

```
export OODA_FS_WRITEDIR="$PWD"
ooda fix hello.oo --yes
```

The human loop is `ooda fix`. HITL is not product.
`fix` patches the file. `patch` replaces one function.

See [Capabilities](Safety.oot) for why writes stay inside `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.

## 6. After the first run

You now have a compiler, a file, a check, a run, and a test.
Add a library with `ooda add hello`. See [Packages](Packages.oot).
Pick a backend or a flag from [Commands](Options.oot).
Read [How it works](Limits.oot) when you want the product path in one place.

